      "sha": "2e5572d79024e9968d37656e0dcb3e7c27556330",
      "author": "DevPrice",
      "summary": "Add guide pitch for vocals practice mode (#1582)",
      "description": "* vibe coded\n\n* refactor\n\n* add basic guide pitch ui\n\n* style\n\n* update guide pitch text color\n\n* hide guide pitch ui if no vocals player\n\n* remove icon ui for now\n\n* improve synth dsp\n\n* handle arbitrary sample rates\n\n* remove clamp\n\n* avoid unsafe in API\n\n* update core\n\n* localize text\n\n* Update core pointer for #397 Separate saved and effective vocal modifiers\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Core pointer update for #420 rid of warnings\n\n* remap guide pitch to vocalist-only orange\n\n* Suppress benign DSP removal error during teardown\n\nGameManager (and its mixer) is destroyed before other GameplayBehaviours,\nso removing the guide pitch DSP in PracticeManager.GameplayDestroy() runs\nafter the mixer stream is already freed. Bass.StreamFree already tears\ndown attached DSPs, so ChannelRemoveDSP failing with Errors.Handle here\nis expected, not a real error — matches the existing pattern used in\nBassOneShotChannel.Dispose() and BassNormalizer for the same scenario.\n\n* Handle AttachOutputDsp failure gracefully in practice guide pitch\n\nBassStemMixer.AttachOutputDsp can return null if the DSP fails to\nattach, but GuidePitchManager's constructor threw on a null handle,\nturning a handled failure into an unhandled exception during\nOnSongStarted(). Bail out and skip the guide pitch feature for that\nsession instead.\n\nAlso bumps YARG.Core to mark AttachOutputDsp's return type as\nnullable, so its declared contract matches BassStemMixer's actual\nbehavior instead of hiding the null case from nullability analysis.\n\n* Use a deliberate 15ms declick fade for guide pitch\n\nFADE_DURATION_SECONDS was 0.00145s (1.45ms) - unit-correct but an\noddly small, non-round value for the note on/off volume envelope,\nflagged in review as likely an accidental slipped decimal. 15ms is\nlong enough to avoid an audible click at note boundaries while\nstaying tight against the vocal note's actual onset.\n\n* Separate the pitch synth DSP from the guide pitch logic\n\nGuidePitchSynthDsp mixed two concerns: sine synthesis and vocal note\nscanning. Split it into SineSynthDsp, a generic tone generator driven by\nan IPitchSource, and VocalNotePitchSource, which owns the part selection\nhandoff and note scan. PracticeManager wires the two together.\n\nNo behavior change.\n\n* fix null reference\n\n* Reimplement guide pitch DSP hook on the new audio pipeline\n\nThe BASS rewrite in \"Implement ASIO support\" (#1587) removed BassStemMixer,\nwhich was where guide pitch attached its sine synth to the output. Port that\nhook onto BassSong.\n\nBassMixerDsp replaces the old BassDspHandle, bridging an IMixerDspProcessor\nonto a BASS mixer's DSP chain. It attaches to BassSongConnection's song mixer,\nthe structural analog of the old output mixer: a float mixer at output rate\nwith the tempo stream as a source channel, so the previous song time\nderivation carries over unchanged. That point is post-tempo, so the tone is\nnever speed- or pitch-shifted, and it sits inside the buffered song branch, so\nthe tone stays sample locked to the music through the read-ahead buffer and\nbehaves the same on ASIO and shared output.\n\nThe song mixer is recreated whenever the output device changes, so BassSong\ntracks its DSPs and BassSongConnection.Create re-attaches them, mirroring how\none-shot channels already handle this.\n\nDetach keeps swallowing Errors.Handle: GameManager.OnDestroy disposes the\nmixer before PracticeManager tears down the guide pitch manager, so detaching\nafter the channel is freed remains the normal teardown path.\n\nSineSynthDsp now scans its song timeline between the buffer's start and end\npositions rather than deriving the span from the sample rate, which removes\nthe intra-buffer drift at reduced practice speeds. Phase still advances in\nreal output time so the tone keeps its written pitch at any speed.\n\n* Address review findings in the guide pitch DSP\n\nCorrectness:\n\n- Guard the processor call against exceptions. An exception unwinding from a\n  managed DSP into its native BASS caller aborts the process, so the processor\n  is latched off and the failure is reported from the main thread instead.\n- Keep the BASS handles when ChannelRemoveDSP fails for a reason other than a\n  stale handle. Clearing them left the DSP attached to a live mixer while the\n  object believed it was detached, so the next attach added a second copy.\n- Release the callback reference only after the DSP has been removed. It is\n  redundant while ManagedBass roots the delegate itself, but the ordering\n  contradicted what it claims to guarantee.\n- Retry registered but unattached DSPs on seek, so an attach that fails during\n  an output device change does not silently disable the effect for the rest of\n  the song.\n\nPerformance:\n\n- Detect a backward seek by comparing against the previously queried song time\n  rather than the start of the upcoming phrase. The scan indices sit on the\n  next phrase while in a gap, so the old test treated every gap longer than the\n  threshold as a seek and rescanned the whole part on every sample.\n- Convert tempo stream bytes to a song position without logging on the audio\n  render thread. YargLogger takes a lock and can block on file I/O, and the\n  failing path could fire once per block.\n\nFixes:\n\n- Move the guide pitch part group activation out of Start(), where iterating\n  GameManager.Players would dereference null: the players are not spawned until\n  the song has loaded.\n\nThe comment justifying the swallowed Errors.Handle described a teardown order\nthat does not happen, since BassSong detaches its DSPs while the mixer is still\nalive. Replaced with the reason the guard is still wanted: Unity does not order\ndestruction between components sharing a GameObject.\n\n* Move the guide pitch synth into the native audio plugin\n\nFollows \"Use native C++ DSP effects\" (#1602), which moved gain, freeverb, and\none-shot scheduling into yarg_audio so that no managed code runs on the\nread-ahead render thread. The guide pitch DSP was the last managed\nChannelSetDSP callback in the project and reintroduced exactly the pattern that\nchange removed: a managed delegate invoked per block on a raw std::thread,\nwhich attaches that thread to the GC and makes every unrelated collection a\ncandidate for a ring buffer underrun.\n\nThe synth now lives in Native/YargAudio/src/dsp/SineSynthDsp.cpp beside the\ngain and freeverb DSPs, and holds the melody as a table of pitch segments. The\nmanaged side publishes that table once when the selection changes, plus a song\ntime offset and playback speed when either moves; the render thread reads the\ntempo stream position itself and needs nothing from managed code.\n\nMoving the scan native also removes the per-sample cost that made it expensive.\nThe old scan restarted whenever the upcoming phrase was more than the backward\nseek threshold away, which is true of any ordinary gap, so it rescanned the\nwhole part on every sample through the gaps that make up most of a vocal chart.\nThe native scan restarts only on a genuine backward jump.\n\nThe Core API is a generic scheduled tone rather than a guide pitch: vocals\nknowledge stays in VocalToneSchedule and GuidePitchManager, which flatten a\nVocalsPart into segments. Only the Windows plugin binary is rebuilt here; the\nLinux and macOS binaries still need a CI build to match the new ABI.\n\n* Remove unrelated scene changes from guide pitch PR\n\nEditor churn had crept into Gameplay.unity alongside the guide pitch UI:\n\n* The Highway Renderer camera's embedded RenderTexture was re-serialized\n  with a fresh fileID on every scene save, and had picked up a\n  window-derived 1469x760 resolution in place of the committed 640x480.\n  Restored the original object and its two references.\n* A ScoreDisplay prefab override reset m_AnchoredPosition.x from\n  -157.1503 to 0, shifting the score display.\n* Five UnisonDisplay fields had been reordered with no value changes.\n\nThe layout group change (m_ChildControlWidth/Height) and the resulting\nchild m_SizeDelta values are kept -- those are needed for the new guide\npitch row to lay out in the practice HUD.\n\nGameplay.unity now differs from dev only by the 16 objects making up the\nguide pitch UI and the practice HUD wiring for them.\n\n* Fade the guide tone out when its schedule is cleared\n\nsineSynthDspProc returned early on an empty note table, before reaching the\nrender loop that owns the declick fade. Toggling the guide pitch off while a\nnote sounded therefore froze currentVolume at full and phase mid-cycle, and the\noutput dropped from full amplitude to zero in a single frame -- exactly the\nclick fadeSeconds exists to prevent.\n\nRe-enabling compounded it: set_notes resets the scan but not the oscillator, so\nthe next tone began at full amplitude from a non-zero phase, breaking the\n\"always starts on a zero crossing\" guarantee.\n\nsineSynthDspFrequencyAt already reports 0 Hz for an empty table, which drives\nthe ramp to zero and resets the phase, so the early-out only ever suppressed\ncorrect behaviour. The managed implementation this replaced had no such\nshortcut.\n\ntestEmptyNoteTableIsSilent covered only a table that was never populated, which\npasses either way; the new test clears the schedule mid-tone and fails without\nthis change.\n\n* Lock the channel when detaching the sine synth DSP\n\nsineSynthDspDetach removed the DSP without holding the channel lock and\ndiscarded the result, unlike every sibling in the plugin: gainDspDestroy,\nfreeverbDspDestroy, NativeOneShotStream::detach and sineSynthDspDestroy all\nlock, remove, unlock and check.\n\nTwo consequences:\n\n* Detach-then-destroy never fenced the render thread. After detach, dsp->dsp\n  was 0, so destroy skipped its own lock/remove block and went straight to\n  delete. Nothing had serialized against a callback that might still be in\n  flight; only the managed disposal order kept that from being reachable.\n\n* Clearing the handles after a failed removal left the proc installed while\n  reporting success. The next set_notes then computed attached == false, so it\n  swapped the note table with no lock at all -- freeing the old vector under a\n  render thread reading it.\n\nDetach now locks around the removal, keeps its handles and reports\nYARG_AUDIO_ERROR_BASS when removal genuinely fails, and treats\nBASS_ERROR_HANDLE as the ordinary teardown path where the channel is already\ngone. destroy delegates to it, so a freed channel also tears down cleanly\ninstead of leaking the object with its proc still registered.\n\nThe entry point gains a bass_error out-param to match\nyarg_one_shot_stream_detach; it was the only one in the header that could\nreturn _ERROR_BASS without a way to diagnose it. The managed wrapper keeps its\nchannel handle on failure so Reattach cannot install a second DSP, matching\nBassNativeOneShotStream.\n\n* Validate the tone schedule before handing it to the render thread\n\nsineSynthDspSetNotes checked only that the pointer was non-null, then copied\nthe segments verbatim, unlike validOneShotSchedule which checks finiteness and\nmonotonicity of its own payload.\n\nA non-finite pitch was the worst case: 440 * pow(2, FLT_MAX) is +inf, which\npasses the frequency > 0 test, drives phase to inf, and makes sin() return NaN\nfor every subsequent sample. Those NaNs are mixed straight into the song mixer\nand propagate through the read-ahead ring to the device, clearing only when the\nnote ends. Out-of-order segments were quieter but still wrong: the render\nthread scans with a forward-only index, so it skips them silently.\n\nSegments must now be finite, non-inverted and sorted by start time, or the\nwhole table is rejected and the previous one stays in place.\n\nAlso restores the guard the managed implementation had: negative pitch is the\nnon-pitched sentinel and must be silence, not a 7.7 Hz rumble that the fade\nnever returns from. VocalToneSchedule filters those notes out, so this is\ndefence in depth rather than a live bug.\n\nTwo related hardening fixes:\n\n* The phase accumulator re-normalized with a single conditional subtract, which\n  only works while the per-sample increment stays below one cycle. It now uses\n  floor, so a frequency above the sample rate cannot make it grow without\n  bound and lose precision in sin().\n* The note_count bound in the C API compared against SIZE_MAX, which is always\n  false on a 64-bit target. It now divides by the element size, as\n  validOneShotCounts does, so a bogus count cannot overflow notes + noteCount.\n\nPitch is deliberately not range-checked: rejecting a whole schedule over one\nodd value would disable the feature outright, and the negative guard plus the\nphase wrap already contain the damage.\n\n* Detect backward seeks exactly instead of by a fixed threshold\n\nThe scan index only ever advanced, and was restarted only when the song time\njumped back by more than 0.5 s. Any shorter rewind left the index parked past\nnotes the player had just seeked back over, so those notes replayed as silence\nuntil playback caught up. Nothing else reset it -- set_timing publishes the new\noffset a seek produces without touching the scan at all.\n\nThe index is stale exactly when the song has moved back before a segment it has\nalready passed, which the previous segment's end time tests directly. That\nneeds no threshold and no separate record of the last serviced time. (Testing\nthe *upcoming* segment's start time, the obvious alternative, would fire on\nevery ordinary gap -- the reason the threshold was there in the first place.)\n\nRepositioning now uses lower_bound rather than restarting from zero, which also\nremoves the one path where the sticky index degraded to O(n) on the render\nthread: a practice section loop, or any replaced table, previously walked the\nwhole song's schedule one segment at a time to catch up.\n\nReplacing the table sets the same reposition flag, so a new schedule seeks to\nthe playhead instead of scanning up to it.\n\n* Accept a larger config struct in the sine synth create call\n\nsineSynthDspCreate rejected any config whose size field did not exactly equal\nthe current struct size. That defeats the purpose of the field: it exists so a\ncaller built against a newer header, with a field appended, still works against\nan older plugin. validOneShotConfig and validReadAheadConfig both use >=, and\nso do the snapshot and stats entry points.\n\nWith ==, the size field was just a second copy of the ABI version, and any\nfuture addition to yarg_sine_synth_config would have been a hard break rather\nthan an additive change.\n\n* Report schedule failures from the native tone channel\n\nFollows the YARG.Core change making ToneChannel.SetSchedule return whether the\nschedule was applied.\n\nyarg_sine_synth_dsp_set_notes was the only entry point in the header that could\nreturn YARG_AUDIO_ERROR_BASS with no bass_error out-param, so the managed log\ncould report result=-5 without saying why the channel lock failed. It now\nmatches the rest of the ABI.\n\nBassToneChannel propagates the result instead of discarding it, and\nGuidePitchManager turns the guide pitch off when a schedule cannot be applied\nrather than leaving the previous section's notes sounding against the chart on\nscreen.\n\n* Marshal tone segments through a struct owned by the BASS layer\n\nFollows the YARG.Core change removing the ABI layout constraint from\nToneSegment.\n\nSetSchedule pinned the Core type and handed the pointer straight to the plugin,\nwhich meant ToneSegment's field order and types were load-bearing for the\nnative ABI. It also worked only implicitly: ToneSegment carried no\nStructLayout attribute and no size assertion, while NativeConfig in this same\nfile already had both.\n\nSegments are now copied into a private NativeSegment that mirrors\nyarg_sine_note, matching how NativeConfig is handled. The copy costs one array\nper schedule change, which happens a few times per song.\n\n* Skip rebuilding the guide pitch schedule when nothing changed\n\nPracticeManager passes VocalTrack.OriginalVocalsTrack, the full-song track,\nwhich is the same object for every section. OnPracticeSectionChanged rebuilt\nand re-pushed a byte-identical schedule on each change: a managed list plus its\nToArray, a native vector copy, a lock on the song mixer -- which the read-ahead\nrender thread is pulling, so the game thread waits for its current block -- and\na scan reposition on the audio thread.\n\nNone of that was needed. The schedule is in absolute song time, so the notes\nalready published serve the new section too. Republish only when the track or\nthe enabled part actually changes, which keeps the method correct if a caller\never does pass a section-limited track.\n\nThe doc comment claimed the schedule follows the new section, which was never\nwhat the code did.\n\n* Remove leftovers from the managed guide pitch DSP\n\nFollows the YARG.Core doc comment cleanup.\n\nAssets/Script/Audio/Effects.meta was orphaned: both files in that folder were\ndeleted with the managed implementation and the directory no longer exists, but\nUnity still tracked the folder's meta and will warn about a meta with no asset.\n\nBassToneChannel.IsAttached had no callers. Reattach consults _dsp.IsAttached\ndirectly; the property was carried over from BassMixerDsp, whose\nRetryDetachedDsps caller no longer exists.\n\n* Name the native tone schedule for what it is, not what uses it\n\nThe rename that moved the managed API from GuidePitch* to Tone* stopped at the\nP/Invoke boundary, leaving the plugin describing a vocals practice feature.\n\nyarg_audio.h documented yarg_sine_note as \"one segment of the guide melody\" --\nthe only documentation that type has, in the public C ABI other consumers read.\nBassCoreBindings, a deliberately consumer-agnostic class, named the guide tone\nDSP as the reason its position bindings exist.\n\nThe vocabulary also disagreed across the boundary: ToneSegment against\nyarg_sine_note, SetSchedule against set_notes. \"Note\" was the wrong word\nregardless, since a pitch slide is two segments rather than two notes.\n\nRenames yarg_sine_note to yarg_tone_segment, set_notes to set_schedule, and\nnote_count to segment_count, and drops the gameplay vocabulary from the\ncomments. The yarg_sine_synth_dsp handle keeps its name: that one describes the\nimplementation, which is accurate and not a layering leak.\n\nThe ABI version is not bumped again -- version 3 is this branch's own\nunreleased surface, so no shipped plugin exports the old names.\n\n* Rebuild the Windows audio plugin\n\nPicks up the sine synth fixes and the renamed set_schedule entry point.\n\nThe Linux and macOS binaries in Assets/Plugins are still stale, as they already\nwere on this branch before these changes: they predate the ABI 3 bump, so the\nmanaged version check rejects them and the guide pitch is silently unavailable\nthere. Both need a native-audio CI run with the artifacts committed back before\nthis branch is mergeable, and --verify-committed-plugin will fail until then.\n\n* Show the guide pitch HUD row again\n\nThe row has been hidden since the activation moved out of Start() into\nOnSongLoaded: SongLoaded is raised at the end of FinalizeChart, which runs\nbefore CreatePlayers, so GameManager.Players is still empty when the handler\nruns. HasVocalsPlayer therefore always returned false and the group was\ndeactivated for the rest of the song, on vocals charts included.\n\nThe comment added at the time -- \"the players are not spawned until the song\nfinishes loading\" -- had the ordering backwards.\n\nActivate from OnSongStarted instead. That event is raised after CreatePlayers,\nand is already the hook PracticeManager uses to build the guide pitch manager,\nso the row and the manager behind it now appear together.\n\n* Show the guide pitch HUD row again\n\nThe row has been hidden since the activation moved out of Start() into\nOnSongLoaded: SongLoaded is raised at the end of FinalizeChart, which runs\nbefore CreatePlayers, so GameManager.Players is still empty when the handler\nruns. HasVocalsPlayer therefore always returned false and the group was\ndeactivated for the rest of the song, on vocals charts included.\n\nThe comment added at the time -- \"the players are not spawned until the song\nfinishes loading\" -- had the ordering backwards.\n\nActivate from OnSongStarted instead. That event is raised after CreatePlayers,\nand is already the hook PracticeManager uses to build the guide pitch manager,\nso the row and the manager behind it now appear together.\n\n* Move guide pitch setup out of PracticeManager\n\nPracticeManager only needs to own the manager's lifetime, not know how one\nis built. Fold the construction preconditions, tuning constants and the\nvocalist-only input gate into GuidePitchManager itself:\n\n  - GuidePitchManager.Create() decides whether guide pitch applies at all\n    (vocals present, tone channel available) and returns null when it does\n    not, so OnSongStarted is a single assignment.\n  - The status callback is passed to Create() instead of being wired up as\n    an event by the caller, which also removes the matching unsubscribe\n    from GameplayDestroy. The manager is the only publisher and the sole\n    owner of the subscription, so there is nothing to leak.\n  - OnToggleRequested() takes the player and ignores non-singers, keeping\n    the instrument check next to the feature it guards.\n\nAlso drop the OnPracticeSectionChanged hook. VocalTrack assigns\n_originalVocalsTrack once during initialization and never replaces it, so\nthe callback always received the same track it already held and returned\nearly without republishing. The schedule is in absolute song time and\nspans the whole chart, so it stays correct across section changes.\n\nNet effect on PracticeManager: 57 changed lines down to 13 added, 0\nremoved. PracticeHud is likewise now purely additive; both files had lost\ntheir UTF-8 BOM (.editorconfig specifies utf-8-bom), which is restored.\n\n* Keep the vocalist check at the guide pitch input site\n\nRouting a guitarist's orange fret into GuidePitchManager and having it\ndecide to do nothing reads as though the press was a guide pitch toggle\nthat happened to be declined. It is not one at all, so the switch arm in\nOnNavigationEvent should not dispatch it in the first place.\n\n* Rebuild the native audio plugins for all platforms\n\n* Route the guide pitch tone to the configured output channels\n\nThe guide pitch tone DSP summed its sample into every channel of the song\nmixer buffer, so on a multichannel device it played from all speakers\ninstead of the default output pair the song audio uses.\n\nGive the sine synth DSP a target output channel (a 1-based speaker-pair\nvalue matching the experimental output-channel setting, 0 = broadcast) and\nrender only into that pair, falling back to every channel for stereo or an\nout-of-range value. BassSong publishes the song's own Master routing to the\ntone channels at creation and whenever it changes, so the tone follows the\nsame speaker pair as the music.\n\n* Core pointer update: merge dev into guide-pitch\n\n* Rebuild the native audio plugins for all platforms\n\n* Show an orange menu-button hint on the guide pitch HUD\n\nGuide pitch is toggled by the orange menu button, but nothing on the HUD\nsays so. Add an Image slot under the guide pitch status that PracticeHud\nfills with the orange menu button's shared sprite and tint, so the control\nis discoverable. The Image is wired in the Gameplay scene; when it is unset\nor the navigation icons are unavailable the hint is simply skipped.\n\n* Add the orange button hint icon to the guide pitch HUD\n\nPlaces the Image the PracticeHud button-hint field drives into the guide\npitch row, and spaces it so it sits the same distance under Off as the\nheader sits above it, with the same gap to the next stat as the other\nrows use.\n\n* Make the guide pitch HUD hint a clickable help bar button\n\nReplace the hand-built pill+dash icon with a runtime instance of the shared HelpBarButton prefab, driven by a MenuAction.Orange nav entry labelled \"Toggle\". This matches the orange menu button everywhere else and makes the hint mouse-clickable, routing through the same ToggleGuidePitch path as the controller's Orange button. A ContentSizeFitter sizes the button to its label so the built-in right padding is preserved. Adds the Gameplay.Practice.GuidePitchToggle localization key.\n\n* Trim redundant comments in PracticeHud\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>"
